{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":242061,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"10898799-ecdb-4e76-9231-882355de06d8","total_cost_usd":1.14148,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":57337,"cache_read_input_tokens":20180,"output_tokens":20682,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":57337,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":20682,"cache_read_input_tokens":20180,"cache_creation_input_tokens":57337,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":57337},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":40865,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.040959999999999996,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":20682,"cacheReadInputTokens":20180,"cacheCreationInputTokens":57337,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.10052,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"The catalog is internally clean and eleven of the value-set rules hold under arithmetic, but the ablation optimum fails when flown (cheapest pass count is 1 in 9 of 9 descents), the pre-staging braking pass is 7x over the cargo-damage threshold, and the staged ballistic coefficient is 6.2x below the physics document the drag ladder came from — with the heat scale itself normalised to the single-plunge trajectory the 2-4 pass rule exists to reject.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_defined_in_baseline\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params appears in the baseline's bands, and no band is referenced that the baseline does not define.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"baseline.bands = {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog debris[].band uses exactly those three (9 pieces each). Params reference suborbital/low/high in ablation.fixed_toll_per_pass_pct_by_band, ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_pass_count and economy.band_value_multiplier — 5 blocks x 3 keys, all matching, no fourth key anywhere and no baseline band unreferenced.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_debris_array\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises: piece_count, mean_mass_kg, fragile_fraction.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recounted and recomputed from the debris array. Suborbital: 9 pieces, mass sum 34+12+28+6.5+9+4+8+5.5+180 = 287, mean 287/9 = 31.89 (claimed 31.89); spawn weight 8+15+6+13+4+10+10+3+1 = 70, fragile weight 4+3 = 7, 7/70 = 0.100 (claimed 0.1). Low: 9 pieces, sum 62+48+22+18+26+11+55+19+260 = 521, mean 57.89 (claimed 57.89); weight 60, fragile 5+3+2 = 10, 10/60 = 0.167 (claimed 0.167). High: 9 pieces, sum 96+55+44+78+21+52+58+68+350 = 822, mean 91.33 (claimed 91.33); weight 64, fragile 6+5+4+1 = 16, 16/64 = 0.250 (claimed 0.25). All nine figures match to the stated precision.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_piece_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises band over band — valuable junk physically fights you.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"31.89 -> 57.89 -> 91.33 kg, monotonic, 2.86x bottom to top. Excluding the one oversized outlier per band, the hand-tetherable population runs 877/8 = 109.6... recomputed per piece: suborbital tetherable mean (34+12+28+6.5+9+4+8+5.5)/8 = 107/8 = 13.38 kg, low (62+48+22+18+26+11+55+19)/8 = 261/8 = 32.63 kg, high (96+55+44+78+21+52+58+68)/8 = 472/8 = 59.00 kg — a 4.41x climb, steeper than the all-piece figure. Neither series is flat or inverted.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted: 7/70 = 0.100 (1 in 10.0), 10/60 = 0.167 (1 in 6.0), 16/64 = 0.250 (1 in 4.0). Monotonic and the two endpoints hit the GDD's 1-in-10 and 1-in-4 exactly. Fragile types also carry below-band-average weights (4,3 | 5,3,2 | 6,5,4,1), so variety rises without inflating encounter rate.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recomputed spawn-weighted over the hand-tetherable suborbital+low population: mass x weight = 877 (suborbital) + 2157 (low) = 3034 kg-weight. Slot cost at compactor_tier 1 with fragile never crushed: crushing every non-fragile piece gives 195 slot-weight -> 15.56 kg/slot, so 6 slots = 93.4 kg and (80+93.4)/80 = 2.17x (+8.5% of doubling). The stricter tier-1 reading (only smalls crush; medium non-fragile = 4 uncrushed slots) gives 100+167 = 267 slot-weight -> 11.36 kg/slot, 6 slots = 68.2 kg, (80+68.2)/80 = 1.85x (-7.5%). Both readings are inside the 25% band, and the simulator's measured full_hold_mass_ratio is 2.17, matching the first.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"ablation_optimum_two_to_four_passes\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The ablation curve is tuned so the cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes, never a single plunge and never feathering.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The array is faithful to its own model and its minima are in range, but the model is not what the game flies. Recomputed cost(n) = n*toll + 0.00074*n*(H/n)^2.2: suborbital n=1 = 2 + 0.00074*25118.9 = 20.59 (array 20.59), n=2 = 4 + 0.00074*2*5467 = 12.09 (12.09), n=3 = 6 + 0.00074*3*2240.5 = 10.97 (10.97), n=4 = 8 + 0.00074*4*1189.9 = 11.52 (11.52); low n=1 = 2.3 + 0.00074*26806 = 22.14 (22.14), n=3 = 6.9 + 0.00074*3*2391.0 = 12.21 (12.21); high n=1 = 2.9 + 0.00074*30979 = 25.83 (25.82), n=3 = 8.7 + 0.00074*3*2763.0 = 14.83 (14.83). All three minima sit at n=3 and equal optimal_pass_count. But the curve's premise — that splitting a descent divides peak heat as H/n — is false in the measured flight results: suborbital empty peaks at 100 for 1 pass and 114 for 2; high empty reads 110/116/115/112/114 across 1/2/3/4/7 passes, a 6% spread that never once falls below the single-pass value. Measured ablation therefore rises monotonically with pass count in every case (suborbital empty 20.6 -> 31.4; low full hold 53.8 -> 69.1 -> 72.7; high full hold 62.9 -> 78.1 -> 84.2 -> 87.3 -> 90.7), and cheapest_pass_count = 1 in 9 of 9 descents. A single plunge is the cheapest descent from every band at every load — the exact outcome the rule forbids.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"No value in the ablation block can fix this — heat_cost_coefficient and the tolls only rescale a curve the simulator never produces; the lever is deceleration authority, so publish a reference_area_m2 in the params near 0.56 (or otherwise raise Cd*A/m) until a shallower pass measurably peaks cooler than a committed one, then re-derive heat_index from those flights.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_full_hold_under_soft_line\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Parachute descent speed at full hold is under the 5 m/s soft-landing threshold and near enough to it that the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"v = sqrt(2*m*g/(rho0*Cd*A)) with m = 80 + 93.4 = 173.4 kg, g = 30, rho0 = 3.0, Cd*A = 1.7*100 = 170: sqrt(10404/510) = sqrt(20.40) = 4.52 m/s analytic; the simulator's integrated touchdown is 4.58 m/s, and landing.descent_speed_full_hold_ms claims 4.6 — all three inside 2% of each other, under soft_landing_ms 5.0 and above the 3.5 floor. The sqrt-of-mass law is visible in the measured series 3.09 (empty) / 3.90 (half) / 4.58 (full), ratios 1.26 and 1.17 against sqrt(m) ratios 1.26 and 1.17. Storage tier 2 (12 slots = 186.7 kg cargo, 266.7 kg all-up) lands at sqrt(16002/510) = 5.60 m/s on the base canopy — hard — and 4.57 m/s on parachute tier 1 (Cd 2.55), so the upgrade buys something specific.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_at_fifty_percent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50%, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"curve = linear, free_radius_m = 160, max_fee_fraction = 0.5. Half the planet's circumference is pi*800 = 2513.27 m. fee(d) = 0.5*(d-160)/(2513.27-160) = 0.5*(d-160)/2353.27: fee(160) = 0, fee(1000) = 0.5*840/2353.27 = 0.1785, fee(2513.27) = 0.5 exactly. Below 160 the numerator is clamped to zero rather than evaluated, and 0.5 is the stated cap, so payout runs 100% at the pad down to 50% at the antipode and never inverts. free_radius_m 160 is also self-consistent with one screen: eva.magnet_range_screens 0.125 at the GDD's ~1/8-screen reach implies a 160 m screen width.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"A fresh stage with a full tank costs about the value of 2-3 pieces of cheap junk, so a lazy run still breaks even.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Cheapest piece in the sky is a suborbital small: size_class_base_value.small 22 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 22. Three of them = 66; launch_cost 48 <= 66, and 48/22 = 2.18 pieces — inside the GDD's 2-3. A genuinely lazy run (6 crushed suborbital smalls in the base 6-slot hold) grosses 132, clears the launch plus the replating of the measured 20.6% single-pass suborbital descent (0.6 x 20.6 = 12.36) with 71.6 left over.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing tension is below the magnet hold force by construction; only jerk shears a magnet.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a = jetpack_thrust_n / (suit_mass_kg + towed mass), per-cable tension = a x that piece's mass, with base_tether_slots 2. Heaviest legal two-piece load in the shipping slice, two satellite_bus_frame at 62 kg: a = 200/(20+124) = 1.389 m/s^2, T = 62 x 1.389 = 86.1 N against magnet_hold_force_n 260 — 3.02x margin. Heaviest anywhere in the catalog, two rtg_core_shell at 96 kg: a = 200/(20+192) = 0.943, T = 90.6 N — 2.87x margin, i.e. adding mass raises per-cable tension more slowly than it suppresses acceleration, exactly as the GDD asserts. Empty-handed the astronaut pulls 200/20 = 10.0 m/s^2 against 1.39 loaded, a 7.2x handling swing. Shear is left to the jerk terms alone: at latch_jerk_coefficient 1.0 a 62 kg piece needs 260/62 = 4.2 m/s of closing speed.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Staging is one-way: no main or RCS thrust remains after jettison.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Every thrust-bearing field in the params belongs to the jettisoned stage or to the suit: flight.thrust_n 7470, flight.rcs_thrust_n 375, flight.fuel_capacity_kg 70 and both burn rates are stage properties, and there is no post-stage thrust, restart, reserve-tank, or residual-RCS field anywhere in flight, reentry, ablation or landing. The only remaining thrust source is eva.jetpack_thrust_n 200, which is the astronaut's get-out-and-push rescue the GDD explicitly permits (200 N against a 173.4 kg staged ship is 1.15 m/s^2 — a nudge, not an engine). No upgrade entry restores thrust either; the twelve effects are fuel_capacity_kg, thrust_n, base_slots, plate_capacity_pct, parachute_drag_coefficient, magnet_range_screens and magnet_hold_force_n.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_and_premium_real\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces are never crushable at any compactor tier present, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier = 1, which by GDD 2.3.4 crushes small only; no params field grants a per-piece or fragile crush exception, and the catalog's size_classes block carries no fragile override — the fragile flag is the only gate and it is set on 9 of 27 pieces. economy.fragile_value_premium = 4: a suborbital small solid is 22 x 1.0 = 22 against a fragile small at 88 (4 solid pieces); a low-band medium solid is 80 x 2.6 = 208 against a fragile medium at 832; a high-band medium is 80 x 6 = 480 against 1920. Four-for-one clears 'several', and the fragile piece additionally pays uncrushed slot cost forever (2 slots small, 4 medium) plus fragile_multiplier 2 on both heat and landing damage, so the premium is paying for real risk.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet and is crane-cable only.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"catalog.size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable = false, against true for small and medium. The three oversized pieces — derelict_upper_stage 180 kg, collapsed_boom_mast 260 kg, armstrongs_satellite 350 kg — are one per band on spawn_weight 1 each, so the slice can ship with them visible and unreachable. Slot costs 6 crushed / 12 uncrushed also exceed the base 6-slot hold, so none of them can be taken on base storage even if a hand tether were somehow granted.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 costing more than tier 1 for the same part, and each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries, 6 parts x 2 tiers: fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet. Costs rise within each part: 180<520, 200<560, 240<700, 260<720, 220<620, 190<540. Stats improve monotonically from base: fuel_capacity_kg 70 -> 92 -> 118; thrust_n 7470 -> 9600 -> 12100; base_slots 6 -> 9 -> 12; plate_capacity_pct 100 -> 140 -> 190; parachute_drag_coefficient 1.7 -> 2.55 -> 4.2 (higher Cd is a lower descent speed, 4.52 -> 3.69 -> 2.87 m/s at full hold). hand_magnet splits its two tiers across the two stats GDD 2.3.6 names for that part — range 0.125 -> 0.1875 screens at tier 1, hold force 260 -> 380 N at tier 2 — each improving the stat its own effect field names.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_bands_populated\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Both shipping-slice bands are reachable and each carries at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes, so the player meets crushing and fragility inside the slice.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 7 small + 1 medium + 1 oversized, fragile pieces cracked_antenna_dish (9 kg) and camera_optics_pod (5.5 kg). Low: 3 small + 5 medium + 1 oversized, fragile pieces cracked_solar_array (22 kg), star_tracker_head (11 kg) and dish_reflector_petal (19 kg). Both bands therefore contain crushable smalls (compactor tier 1) and uncrushable fragiles, and both are defined in baseline.bands with sub-60 s periods (45.4 s and 55.2 s at midpoint, 59.6 s at the low band ceiling). Measured ascents confirm reachability: suborbital arrives with 13.21 kg (18.9%) and low with 8.99 kg (12.8%), both above the ~8% the deorbit burn needs.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved_to_satisfy_a_rule\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes because the quantity it is measured against was moved rather than the design tuned.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Every quantity the GDD fixes in prose survives intact — base_slots 6 (§2.2 'Cargo reads 4 of 6'), base_tether_slots 2, soft_landing_ms 5.0, magnet_range_screens 0.125, compactor_tier 1, no_gear_multiplier 2, fragile_multiplier 2, cargo_damage_interval_s 3, heat_dissipation_s 5, max_fee_fraction 0.5, heat_capacity 100, twelve purchases. The moved target is the heat scale itself. The measured results state the 0-100 bar is normalised so that an EMPTY SINGLE-PASS descent from the suborbital band reads exactly 100, and ablation.heat_index is then set to 100/103/110 — the measured single-pass peaks of the three bands, matching to the digit. The ablation rule that forbids a single plunge is therefore scored on a scale whose unit is defined by a single plunge: n=1 is the reference by construction, and the coolest multi-pass descent measured anywhere is 112 (high band, empty, 4 passes), never below the reference in any of the nine descents. That is why no coefficient in the ablation block moves the flown optimum, and it is a separate defect from the curve failure above.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Re-anchor ablation.heat_index to a reference trajectory that is not the trajectory under test — normalise the 0-100 bar on a fixed reference pass geometry (e.g. a stated periapsis and entry speed) rather than on the empty single-pass descent, so pass count is free to move the measurement.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"unstaged_braking_pass_survivable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Shallow unstaged passes are survivable because cargo damage only begins at 100% heat — the intended descent opens with coarse braking before staging, while thrust remains to shape the orbit between passes.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Measured shallow unstaged passes peak at 687 (suborbital) and 719 (low) on the 0-100 bar, i.e. 6.9x and 7.2x the cargo-damage threshold, with survivable = false in both. At cargo_damage_interval_s 3 the hold is being destroyed a slot every 3 seconds throughout a phase the GDD describes as the cheap opening of the descent. reentry.unstaged_heat_multiplier 3.2 is not the cause: 687/3.2 = 215, still 2.15x over the cap, so even at a multiplier of 1.0 the phase is unflyable. The mechanism is the unstaged ballistic coefficient of 50.8 kg/m^2 with Cd 0.45 — the hull lingers in the air long enough for the 5-second heat bar to reach equilibrium, which the staged shield's committed plunge never does. As specified, the player cannot fly the coarse-braking phase at all and must plunge, which is the same defect the ablation curve fails on, seen from the other side.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Lower reentry.unstaged_heat_multiplier toward 1.0 as a necessary but insufficient first move, and pair it with a shorter time-at-altitude for the naked hull — a smaller published reference_area_m2 or lower dry_mass_kg — until a shallow unstaged pass peaks under 100.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"ballistic_coefficient_matches_physics_baseline\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"The params and the physics baseline must describe the same vehicle — the baseline's drag ladder and 3.2x staging gain are derived from a stated mass and reference area.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"baseline.json derives 2.9 / 15.0 / 111 m/s of delta-v per pass from a 500 kg ship at reference_area_m2 3.5, giving m/(Cd*A) = 500/(1.45*3.5) = 98.5 kg/m^2 staged and 500/(0.45*3.5) = 317 unstaged. The ship actually flown is dry_mass_kg 80 at the same 3.5 m^2: measured staged 15.8 and unstaged 50.8 kg/m^2 — 6.2x below the document the ladder came from, and at or below the ~50 floor the simulator names as the point where the atmosphere stops the ship rather than braking it. Reproducing 98.5 at 80 kg needs reference_area_m2 = 80/(1.45*98.5) = 0.560 m^2, a 0.84 m ship. The params block publishes no reference area at all, so the discrepancy is currently invisible to the code that will read it.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Publish reference_area_m2 in the params near 0.56 rather than inheriting the baseline's 3.5, so the flown 80 kg ship reproduces the baseline's own 98.5 kg/m^2 staged ballistic coefficient — raising dry_mass_kg to 500 instead would break full_hold_doubles_mass against this catalog.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The return leg has no failure mode. All nine measured descents report landed, soft_landing and plate_survives true — including the worst legal case, a full hold from the high band on a base 100% plate, which costs 62.9% and touches down at 4.58 m/s. Greed is priced (suborbital empty-to-full moves ablation 20.6 -> 50.2% and peak heat 100 -> 154) but never reaches a threshold that costs the player anything. §4.5's central bet is that the return leg gets harder as hauls improve; right now it gets more expensive and stays safe.\",\n    \"The soft-landing margin is 0.42 m/s (4.58 against 5.0, 8%). At g = 30 that is 0.42 m of free fall, so any bounce, late chute release or gear-deploy slip is an automatic hard landing with no intermediate outcome. This is the design working as intended only if the last half-metre is meant to be all-or-nothing.\",\n    \"tungsten_ballast_slug is 55 kg in one crushed slot — 3.5x the 15.56 kg/slot slice average. Six of them is a legal 6-slot hold weighing 330 kg against an 80 kg ship (5.1x), which descends at sqrt(2*410*30/(3*170)) = 7.36 m/s on the base canopy and 4.74 m/s only at parachute tier 2. A base-storage hold that cannot be landed softly at any purchasable upgrade in the slice is worth confirming as intentional rather than merely expensive.\",\n    \"The high band is reachable at 6.0% fuel remaining (4.17 kg) against the ~8% the deorbit burn needs, so a base ship that gets there is stranded. That is the GDD's own loss condition working, and it is currently the only place in the numbers where a player can actually die — which means the game kills you on the way up, never on the way down.\",\n    \"Band difficulty comes entirely from mass, not altitude, and the params are honest about it: heat_index spans 100/103/110, a 10% spread across all three bands, while empty-to-full-hold at fixed band moves peak heat 100 -> 154, a 54% spread. Full-hold single-pass ablation is 50.2 / 53.8 / 62.9% — the whole three-band climb is worth less than one hold of cargo. §2.3.7's 'each band up is disproportionately hotter' is not true of this value set, and the Researcher's own note says orbital geometry cannot make it true; band-gated ablation tolls (currently only 2.0 / 2.3 / 2.9) are the lever that could.\",\n    \"reentry.off_retrograde_penalty is 1, i.e. no penalty at all for drifting off-retrograde. GDD 2.3.1 says the shield protects the shield-facing side only and heat should build faster by the cosine of the angle. Holding attitude through the plasma is currently free, which removes the one thing the player is actually doing during the hottest seconds of the flight.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":133289,"ttft_stream_ms":2408,"time_to_request_ms":478,"type":"result","duration_ms":240513,"uuid":"023e6009-9b5d-4a77-8f14-05faf44f6769"}
